     "title": [
      {
       "type": "heading2",
       "text": "Easing",
       "spans": [],
       "direction": "ltr"
      }
     ],
     "ingress_title": [
      {
       "type": "heading2",
       "text": " ",
       "spans": [],
       "direction": "ltr"
      }
     ],
     "body": [
      {
       "type": "paragraph",
       "text": "Easing in animation refers to the way an animation progresses through its duration, affecting the motion's speed and acceleration. Instead of moving at a constant pace, easing functions create more natural and realistic movements by varying the rate of change.",
       "spans": [],
       "direction": "ltr"
      },
      {
       "type": "paragraph",
       "text": "The Aalto design system defines the following easing tokens:\n- In: The animation starts slowly and accelerates towards the end. This is often used to simulate the way objects gain speed gradually.\n- Out: The animation starts quickly and decelerates towards the end, mimicking the way objects slow down before coming to a stop.\n- In Out: The animation starts slowly, speeds up in the middle, and then slows down again towards the end, providing a smooth and natural transition.",
       "spans": [
